**user**:
> How do I resolve this:
> PyError (PyImport_ImportModule) <class 'ValueError'>
> ValueError('numpy.dtype size changed, may indicate binary incompatibility. Expected 96 from C header, got 88 from PyObject')
**assistant**:
> This means a compiled package (often `pandas`, `scipy`, or a custom extension) was built against a different NumPy version than the one currently installed.
> 
> For **Julia `PyCall` using its default Conda environment**, the cleanest fix is:
> 
> ```julia
> using Conda
> 
> Conda.runconda(`remove -y numpy pandas`)
> Conda.runconda(`clean --all -y`)
> Conda.runconda(`install -y numpy pandas`)
> ```
> 
> Then rebuild PyCall and restart Julia:
> 
> ```julia
> using Pkg
> Pkg.build("PyCall")
> ```
> 
> If it still fails, reset PyCall’s managed environment completely:
> 
> ```julia
> using Conda
> rm(Conda.ROOTENV; recursive=true, force=true)
> 
> using Pkg
> Pkg.build("PyCall")
> ```
> 
> Then reinstall the Python packages you need:
> 
> ```julia
> using Conda
> Conda.add("numpy")
> Conda.add("pandas")
> ```
> 
> If PyCall is configured to use a **system Python or virtualenv** rather than Conda, activate that same environment and reinstall NumPy plus the failing compiled package together:
> 
> ```bash
> python -m pip install --upgrade --force-reinstall --no-cache-dir numpy pandas
> ```
> 
> Avoid mixing `pip` and `conda` for core compiled packages in the same environment unless necessary.
